's okay.\n\nNow we have covered A3, A4, B1, B2. Remaining points: B3=(n-2,3), C1=(n-1,1), C2=(n-1,2). We have one line left (L4) to cover these three points. Let's see if they are collinear. Points (n-2,3), (n-1,1), (n-1,2) cannot be collinear because (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) are vertical line x=n-1, slope infinite, not allowed. So we cannot have a line covering both C1 and C2. So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nWe could try to pair C1 with B3: line through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1) slope = (1-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -2/1 = -2, allowed. That line also passes through (n-3,?) maybe? Let's compute at x = n-3: y = 3 + slope*(n-3 - (n-2)) = 3 + (-2)*(-1) = 5. That's not in column n-3 (max y=4). So line through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1) only covers those two points. So we could assign L4 to cover (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). Then we still need to cover (n-1,2). That point would need to be covered by one of the previous lines. Let's see if any of the previous lines can be adjusted to include (n-1,2). For example, line through (n-3,2) and (n-2,? )? Actually we used (n-3,2) already in L1 (which covered (n,1) and (n-3,2)). L1 cannot be changed because it covers (n,1). But maybe we could choose L1 to be (n,1)-(n-3,2) as before, which does not intersect column n-1 at integer point. So L1 cannot cover (n-1,2). L2 (covering (n-3,3) and (n-2,1)) does not intersect column n-1 at integer point. L3 (covering (n-3,4) and (n-2,2)) also does not intersect column n-1 at integer point. L4 (covering (n-2,3) and (n-1,1)) does not cover (n-1,2). So (n-1,2) remains uncovered. So this assignment fails.\n\nWe need to adjust to ensure (n-1,2) is covered. Perhaps we can pair (n-1,2) with (n-3,? ) or (n-2,?) in a line.\n\nLet's try to pair (n-1,2) with (n-3,1). Slope = (2-1)/(n-1-(n-3)) = 1/2 = 0.5, allowed (not 0,∞,-1). So line through (n-3,1) and (n-1,2) slope 1/2. Equation: y - 1 = 1/2 (x - (n-3)). At x = n-2: y = 1 + 1/2 (n-2 - (n-3)) = 1 + 1/2 (1) = 1.5, not integer. So this line does not intersect column n-2 at integer point. So it would cover only (n-3,1) and (n-1,2). That's okay.\n\nNow we need to cover (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3), (n-1,1), and (n-3,2), (n-3,3), (n-3,4). We have L1 covering (n,1) and (n-3,2). L2 covering (n-3,3) and (n-2,1) (slope -2). L3 covering (n-3,4) and (n-2,2) (slope -2). L4 covering (n-3,1) and (n-1,2) (slope 1/2). We still need to cover (n-1,1) and (n-2,3). We have used all 4 lines. So we need to adjust again.\n\nMaybe we can pair (n-1,1) with (n-2,3). Slope = (1-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -2/1 = -2, allowed. So line through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1) slope -2. That line also passes through (n-3,5) which is outside. So we could use that line to cover (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). Then we need to cover (n-3,1) maybe with a line that also covers something else.\n\nLet's try to reassign:\n\n- L1: (n,1)-(n-3,2) slope -1/3 (covers D and A2)\n- L2: (n-3,3)-(n-2,1) slope -2 (covers A3 and B1)\n- L3: (n-3,4)-(n-2,2) slope -2 (covers A4 and B2)\n- L4: (n-2,3)-(n-1,1) slope -2 (covers B3 and C1)\n\nNow we have covered all points except (n-1,2) and (n-3,1). We have no lines left. So we need to adjust to include those.\n\nWe could try to modify L1 to also cover (n-1,2) maybe? Let's see if line through (n,1) and (n-3,2) also passes through (n-1,2). At x = n-1, y = 1 - 1/3 (n-1 - n) = 1 - 1/3 (-1) = 1 + 1/3 = 4/3, not 2. So no.\n\nWe could try to modify L2 to also cover (n-1,2) maybe? L2 is line through (n-3,3) and (n-2,1). At x = n-1, y =? Let's compute slope = (1-3)/(n-2-(n-3)) = -2/1 = -2. Equation: y - 3 = -2 (x - (n-3)). At x = n-1: y - 3 = -2 (n-1 - (n-3)) = -2 (2) = -4 => y = -1, not 2. So no.\n\nL3: line through (n-3,4) and (n-2,2) slope = (2-4)/1 = -2. At x = n-1: y - 4 = -2 (n-1 - (n-3)) = -2*2 = -4 => y = 0, not 2.\n\nL4: line through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1) slope -2. At x = n-3: y - 3 = -2 (n-3 - (n-2)) = -2 (-1) = 2 => y = 5, not 1.\n\nThus none of these lines can be adjusted to include (n-1,2) or (n-3,1). So we need a different assignment.\n\nPerhaps we can use a line with slope 1/2 to