east one line; it doesn't forbid a point being on multiple lines. However, lines must be distinct. So we can have a sunny line that passes through (n-1,2) and some other point (a,b) that is already covered by a vertical line. That would be okay. But we need to ensure that the sunny line is not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or slope -1. So we need a line with slope not 0, ∞, -1. For example, line through (n-1,2) and (1,1) has slope (2-1)/(n-1-1) = 1/(n-2). That's not 0, ∞, -1 (provided n>2). So that's a sunny line. So we can have a sunny line connecting (n-1,2) to (1,1). That line will also pass through some other points maybe, but that's okay. So we have a covering: vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-2 (n-2 lines), horizontal line y=1 covering (n-1,1) and (n,1) (non-sunny), and sunny line through (n-1,2) and (1,1). That's total n lines, exactly one sunny line. All points are covered: vertical lines cover all points in columns 1 to n-2; horizontal line covers (n-1,1) and (n,1); sunny line covers (n-1,2) and (1,1) (but (1,1) already covered by vertical line x=1). So all points covered. So k=1 works.\n\nThus k=1 is possible.\n\nk=2: The solution claims impossible. Let's test if we can find a covering with exactly two sunny lines. Perhaps we can try to construct. The solution's argument: Column x=n-2 has 3 points, forcing a vertical line. Then analyzing columns x=n-1 and x=n shows that point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny). This forces a third sunny line, contradicting k=2. Let's examine that argument.\n\nFirst, column x=n-2 has points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3) because a+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ n+1-(n-2) = 3. So column n-2 has three points. If we have only two sunny lines, we need to cover these three points. Since a sunny line can intersect a column at at most one point (since a sunny line is not vertical, not horizontal, not slope -1). Actually, a sunny line can intersect a vertical line