t 3 points per column (one per line). So column 1 can be covered by at most 3 points (good, we have exactly 3). Column 2 can be covered by at most 3 points (we have 2). Column 3 can be covered by at most 3 points (we have 1). So three sunny lines could potentially cover all remaining points if we can assign them appropriately. Let's try to find three sunny lines that cover these points.\n\nWe need to assign each point to one of the three sunny lines, ensuring each line picks at most one point per column. So we need to partition the 6 points into three groups, each group having at most one point from each column.\n\nOne possible grouping: L1: (1,2) and (2,3) and (3,2)? Wait (3,2) is column 3, okay. L1 would have points (1,2) (col1), (2,3) (col2), (3,2) (col3). That's okay: one per column. L2: (1,3) (col1), (2,2) (col2). L3: (1,4) (col1). But L2 has two points from columns 1 and 2, okay. L3 has one point from column 1. So each line has at most one per column. So we can find lines passing through those points. Need to check if there exist lines (not parallel to axes or slope -1) that pass through each group of points. For L1: points (1,2),(2,3),(3,2). Are they collinear? Let's compute slopes: slope between (1,2) and (2,3) = (3-2)/(2-1)=1. Slope between (2,3) and (3,2) = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1. So they are not collinear. So we cannot have a single line passing through all three points. But we can assign each line to a set of points that are collinear. Actually each sunny line must be a line, so each line can only contain points that are collinear. So we need to partition points into at most three lines, each line being a line (i.e., all points assigned to a line must be collinear). So we need to find three lines (sunny) that cover the remaining points. This is more restrictive.\n\nThus we need to find a configuration of one non-sunny line and three sunny lines that cover all points. Let's try to find a known solution. The solution claims k=3 works for any n≥3. So there must b